          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Categorical compositional distributional semantics, also known as
            %strong DisCoCat
            for short, uses category theory to combine the benefits of two very different approaches to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linguistics"} linguistics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorial+grammar"} categorial grammar
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributional+semantics"} distributional semantics
          %p
            Specifically, it uses the fact that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pregroups"} pregroups
            and the category of finite-dimensional real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector spaces
            are both examples of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+monoidal+category"} rigid monoidal categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pregroup+grammars"} pregroup grammars
            may then be given as a strong
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functor"} monoidal functor
          %p
            DisCoCat was first introduced in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Coecke10"} Coecke, Sadrzadeh and Clark 2010
          %p
            There is a curious (if probably shallow) analogy between DisCoCat and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theory"} topological quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+monoidal+category"} rigid monoidal
            or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed
          %h2#simple_example Simple example
          %p
            Consider a very simple fragment of English consisting of nouns and verbs. Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            for the type of nouns, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            for the type of sentences. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            be the free pregroup generated by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            and a reflexive verb will be assigned the type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi n
                      %mi r
                    %mi s
                    %msup
                      %mi n
                      %mi l
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n^{r} s n^{l}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi n
                    %mi r
                  %mi s
                  %msup
                    %mi n
                    %mi l
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n n^{r} s n^{l} n \leq s
          %p in the free pregroup.
          %p
            A strong monoidal functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi FVect
                    %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : \mathcal{C} \to FVect_{\mathbb{R}}
            is completely determined by where it sends the generators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F (n)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F (s)
            are called the
            %em noun space
            and the
            = succeed "," do
              %em sentence space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %em basis words
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1000
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 1000
            (or as many as your sparse matrix implementation can handle), and take
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 1000
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F (n) = \mathbb{R}^{1000}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F (s)
            is not well understood, but
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F (s) = \mathbb{R}^{2}
            is a common choice, with the standard basis vectors interpreted as “true” and “false”.
          %p
            Given a grammatical parsing such as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %msup
                      %mi n
                      %mi r
                    %mi s
                    %msup
                      %mi n
                      %mi l
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n n^{r} s n^{l} n \leq s
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi n
                    %mi r
                  %mi s
                  %msup
                    %mi n
                    %mi l
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F (n n^{r} s n^{l} n) : F (n) \otimes F (n) \otimes F (s) \otimes F (n) \otimes F (n) \to F (s)
          %p given by appropriate tensor contractions.
          %p
            The final ingredient we need are
            = succeed "." do
              %em word vectors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Alice
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Bob
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\![ Alice ]\!], [\![ Bob ]\!] \in F (n)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi loves
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\![ loves ]\!] \in F (n) \otimes F (s) \otimes F (n)
          %p Finally, we obtain a sentence-vector
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi n
                    %mi r
                  %mi s
                  %msup
                    %mi n
                    %mi l
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Alice
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi loves
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Bob
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F (n n^{r} s n^{l} n \leq s) ([\![ Alice ]\!] \otimes [\![ loves ]\!] \otimes [\![ Bob ]\!]) \in F (s)
          %h2#relative_pronouns_and_frobenius_algebras Relative pronouns and Frobenius algebras
          %h2#free_pregroups_vs_free_rigid_monoidal_categories Free pregroups vs free rigid monoidal categories
          %p
            All earlier papers on DisCoCat use free pregroups for the grammar category, building on earlier work by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joachim+Lambek"} Joachim Lambek
            on pregroup grammars. Unfortunately, in
            %a{:href => "#Preller14"} Preller 2014, fact 1
            it is proven that any strong monoidal functor from a free pregroup to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FVect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FVect
            is necessarily trivial, in the sense that it sends every generator to the monoidal unit. One should instead use a free
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+monoidal+category"} rigid monoidal category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+relevance"} proof relevant
            grammatical reductions. They may be encoded as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pregroup+grammar"} pregroup grammar
          %p
            This allows a slightly more elegant reformulation of our basic example. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            be the free autonomous category on the objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n, s
            and the morphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Alice
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Alice : 1 \to n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bob
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bob : 1 \to n
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi loves
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi n
                      %mi r
                    %mi s
                    %msup
                      %mi n
                      %mi l
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} loves : 1 \to n^{r} s n^{l}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : 1 \to s
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Alice
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Alice
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\![ Alice ]\!] = F (Alice)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Bob
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Bob
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\![ Bob ]\!] = F (Bob)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi loves
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi loves
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\![ loves ]\!] = F (loves)
            become part of the data defining
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo →
                    %mi FVect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : \mathcal{C} \to FVect
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F (f)
          %h2#the_category_of_discocat_models The Category of DisCoCat Models
          %p
            One criticism of DisCoCat is that a single model is unlikely to be complex enough to capture the intricacies of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+language"} natural language
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+language"} natural language
            %em
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.11041"} Translating and Evolving: Towards a Model of Language Change in DisCoCat
            the authors construct a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DisCoCat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DisCoCat
            of DisCoCat models where
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                objects are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monoidal+functors"} strong monoidal functors
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi J
                      %mo ,
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi FVect
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon (J,*) \to (FVect,\otimes)
              %p
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi J
                      %mo ,
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (J,*)
                is a category which is freely monoidal on some set of grammatical types. In practice
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
                might be freely equipped with an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+monoidal+category"} rigid
                structure or a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+algebra"} Frobenius algebra
                on each object.
            %li
              %p
                A morphism from a language model
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi J
                      %mo →
                      %mi FVect
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon J \to FVect
                to a language model
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi J
                      %mo ′
                      %mo →
                      %mi FVect
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F' \colon J' \to FVect
                is a tuple
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi j
                      %mo ,
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (j , \alpha)
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functor"} monoidal functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi J
                      %mo →
                      %mi J
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \colon J \to J'
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
