                %path{:d => "M 81.065406 -5.997875 L 81.073219 -5.978344 ", :style => "fill:none;stroke-width:0.478;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke:rgb(0%,0%,0%);stroke-opacity:1;stroke-miterlimit:10;", :transform => "matrix(1,0,0,-1,10.126,11.424)"}
          %p
            Morphisms in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DisCoCat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DisCoCat
            are called translations because they represent ways of changing your semantic interpretations of grammatical types in a way which is coherent with a change in grammar.
          %h3#the_product_space_representation The Product Space Representation
          %p
            In
            %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.4394"}
              %em Mathematical foundations for a compositional distributional model of meaning
            a different description of a DisCoCat model was described. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            be a free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/autonomous+category"} autonomous category
            representing grammar and assume that you have an assignment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_a
            of each grammatical type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            to a finite dimensional vector space. Then grammatical computations of meaning can be performed in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+category"} product category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mo ×
                    %mi FVect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J \times FVect
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi …
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_1 \cdot \ldots \cdot a_n
            be an object in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi …
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in V_{a_1} \otimes \ldots \otimes V_{a_n}
            be its semantic meaning. Then a grammatical reduction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon a \to b
            built from the units and counits in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            can be paired with the corresponding structure maps in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi FVect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FVect
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            to the vector
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            to get a new semantic meaning. In essence, this approach identifies an autonomous subcategory of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                  %mo ×
                  %mi FVect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J \times FVect
            where meaning computations can occur.
          %p
            This product space representation can be related to monoidal functor perspective via the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+elements"} category of elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi FVect
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \colon FVect \to Set
            the product space of a language model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi J
                  %mo →
                  %mi FVect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon J \to FVect
            can be defined as the category of elements
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int i \circ F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            is a set of words then a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo →
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi i
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \to \int i \circ F
            describes an assignment of each word to a grammatical type and semantic meaning.
          %h2#variations Variations
          %p
            DisCoCat is relatively easy to modify by replacing the semantic category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FVect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FVect
            with some other category with the appropriate structure. Examples include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"}> Relations
                , which yields a semantics in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+logic"} regular logic
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+bicategory"} Cartesian bicategories
                %a{:href => "#Felice19"} Felice, Meichanetzidis and Toumi 2019
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density+matrix"} Density matrices
                to model semantic ambiguity such as “financial bank” vs “river bank”
            %li
              %p
                Convex relations (i.e.
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relations
                in the category of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+space"} convex spaces
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Bolt16"} Bolt et al 2016
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/game+theory"} Games
                to model dialogue and Wittgenstein’s
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/language+game"} language games
                (
                %a{:href => "#Hedges18"}> Hedges and Lewis 2018
                )
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/DisCoPy"} DisCoPy
          %h1#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2018/02/linguistics_using_category_the.html"} Linguistics using category theory
                on the n-category café
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2018/03/cognition_convexity_and_catego.html"} Cognition, convexity and category theory
                on the n-category café
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2019/06/meeting_the_dialogue_challenge.html"} Meeting the Dialogue Challenge
                on the n-category café
            %li#Coecke10
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bob+Coecke"}> Bob Coecke
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mehrnoosh+Sadrzadeh"} Mehrnoosh Sadrzadeh
                and Stephen Clark,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Mathematical foundations for a compositional distributional model of meaning
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.4394"} arXiv:1003.4394
            %li#Preller14
              %p
                Anne Preller,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em From logical to distributional methods
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.8527"} arXiv:1412.8527
            %li#Bolt16
              %p
                Josef Bolt,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bob+Coecke"} Bob Coecke
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Interacting conceptual spaces I: Grammatical composition of concepts
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.08314"} arXiv:1703.08314
            %li#Felice19
              %p
                Giovanni de Felice, Konstantinos Meichanetzidis and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexis+Toumi"} Alexis Toumi
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Functorial Question Answering
                = succeed "])" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.07408"} arXiv:1905.07408
            %li#Hedges18
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jules+Hedges"} Jules Hedges
                and Martha Lewis,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Towards functorial language-games
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.07828"} arXiv:1807.07828
          %p
            The category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DisCoCat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DisCoCat
            and it’s relationship to the product space representation is discussed in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tai-Danae+Bradley"}> Tai-Danae Bradley
              , Martha Lewis,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jade+Master"} Jade Master
              %p
                %em Translating and Evolving: Towards a Model of Language Change in DisCoCat
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.11041"} arXiv:1811.11041
